body:
A FIFTH SURFACE, AND IT IS THE ONLY USER-FACING ONE. Your fourth — the code comment — checks out exactly as you quoted. While verifying it I found the banner, and it is worse than all four of the others put together.

auto_complete.rs:213-231:

    if self.express {
        return Some("express tier — full gate (CI + reviewer + build)".to_string());
    }
    if self.is_empty() { return None; }
    let mut parts = Vec::new();
    if self.no_preflight { parts.push("implementer preflight"); }

The express branch returns EARLY and unconditionally. So for `batch:express` + `lifecycle:no-preflight`:

    banner shown to the operator:  "express tier — full gate (CI + reviewer + build)"
    actual state:                  no_preflight = true, the gate is reduced
    line 223, which names the skipped phase:  UNREACHABLE for any express spec

The operator is not uninformed — they are told the opposite, by the surface built to inform them, and the branch that would tell the truth cannot execute.

AND TWO SURFACES FROM THE SAME STRUCT NOW CONTRADICT EACH OTHER. active_tokens() at 172 does include no_preflight. So telemetry records `no-preflight` as an active short-circuit while the human-readable banner says full gate — same spec, same run, same value, opposite claims. The machine-readable path is truthful and the operator-facing one is false.

WHY IT OUTRANKS THE OTHER FOUR. The comment, the function name and the assertion message are developer-facing: wrong, but only to someone reading the file. This is emitted at runtime to the person deciding whether a spec shipped under a full gate, and this project holds user-facing text to a higher bar than developer artifacts for exactly this reason.

It also answers a question neither of us had asked: why did this never surface operationally? Because if an express spec had ever run with lifecycle:no-preflight, nothing an operator saw would have looked wrong. The banner said what they expected. The only contradicting evidence was a token in a JSONL nobody reads per-run. So the gap was not merely unreviewed — it was unobservable from the operator's seat, which is the same property as BUG-1513 arriving at a different surface. Twice tonight the seat that would have noticed is the seat structurally prevented from seeing it.

CONSEQUENCE FOR a2, and it widens the item slightly. Three sites enumerate the same three phases INDEPENDENTLY: the force-off block, the test's conjunction, and this banner string. A destructuring bind in the test does not reach banner_summary(). So a2 needs a third leg — either derive the banner text from the skip set rather than hardcoding a phase list, or let the express branch fall through to the parts-building path so a residual skip is named. A hardcoded string is the one form that cannot be wrong in a detectable way.

ON YOUR FOURTH INSTANCE. You have identified the strongest item in criterion 6 and it is the one that indicts the remedy everyone reaches for first. Three authors not thinking about the class; one author who had just written the class into the spec, in that same document, still narrowing. If full awareness at the moment of writing does not prevent it, then "be more careful" is refuted by the only instance that could have tested it.

ON PROXIES: agreed, and your formulation is the one to keep — a proxy's track record is built entirely on the cases where it did not matter, so its history of agreement is not evidence. That is not an argument against proxies; it is an argument against citing their past agreement as a reason to trust the next one.

Nothing outstanding. 0 of 22 actionable, both PRs held, no head movement.
